<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Embroidered Uniform Guard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1368">1368</a>–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jinyiwei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Embroidered Uniform Guard, or Jinyiwei, was the imperial secret police of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> in 1368 as his personal bodyguard and formally established in 1382 with about 500 members, who grew to around 14,000 in three years. Taking orders from the emperor alone, it could overrule judicial proceedings and arrest, interrogate, detain without trial and punish anyone. Its duties were curtailed in 1393 after it allegedly abused its authority in the case of the general Lan Yu, in which about 40,000 people were executed, and restored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>. It was disbanded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels overthrew the Ming in 1644.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/embroidered-uniform-guard.jpg" alt="Embroidered Uniform Guard" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinyiwei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
